@startuml Permission Marketing MCP System
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam componentStyle rectangle

title Permission Marketing MCP: From Interruption to Delegation\n(Based on Seth Godin's Permission Marketing Framework)

' ============================================================================
' LEGEND & COLOR SCHEME
' ============================================================================
legend top
|= Level |= Permission Type |= Color |= Auto-Grant |= Trust Required |
| **1** | Situational | <#90EE90> | YES | None |
| **2** | Brand Trust | <#FFFFE0> | NO | Explicit Opt-in |
| **3** | Personal Relationship | <#FFD700> | NO | Authentication |
| **4** | Points Permission | <#FFA500> | NO | Loyalty Program |
| **5** | Agentic/Intravenous | <#FF6B6B> | NO | Delegation + Constraints |

**Key Concept:** Each level represents earned permission, not assumed rights.
The agent climbs the ladder by delivering value and earning trust.
endlegend

' ============================================================================
' PART 1: ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW
' ============================================================================
package "Permission Marketing MCP Server" as MCPServer {
    
    frame "MCP Tools" as Tools {
        component "request_permission()" as ReqPerm <<tool>> #LightBlue
        note right of ReqPerm
          scope: str
          reason: str
          duration: str
          constraints: dict
          Returns: PermissionGrant
        end note
        
        component "check_permission()" as CheckPerm <<tool>> #LightBlue
        component "revoke_permission()" as RevokePerm <<tool>> #LightBlue
        component "list_permissions()" as ListPerm <<tool>> #LightBlue
        component "escalate_permission()" as EscalatePerm <<tool>> #LightBlue
        component "explain_permission()" as ExplainPerm <<tool>> #LightBlue
    }
    
    frame "MCP Resources" as Resources {
        component "resource://permissions/\n{user_id}/current" as CurrentRes <<resource>> #LightGreen
        component "resource://permissions/\n{user_id}/ladder" as LadderRes <<resource>> #LightGreen
        component "resource://permissions/\n{user_id}/audit" as AuditRes <<resource>> #LightGreen
    }
    
    frame "MCP Prompts" as Prompts {
        component "permission_request_template" as ReqPrompt <<prompt>> #LightYellow
        component "value_exchange_template" as ValuePrompt <<prompt>> #LightYellow
        component "denial_handler_template" as DenialPrompt <<prompt>> #LightYellow
    }
    
    database "Permission Database" as PermDB {
        frame "Tables" {
            [permissions]
            [permission_grants]
            [permission_audit_log]
            [permission_ladder_config]
        }
    }
}

actor "User" as User
component "AI Agent\n(Shopping Assistant)" as Agent
cloud "User Interface\n(Chat/Voice)" as UI

User -down-> UI : converses
UI -down-> Agent : intents & responses
Agent -down-> MCPServer : MCP JSON-RPC
MCPServer -right-> PermDB : read/write state

' ============================================================================
' PART 2: PERMISSION LADDER MAPPING
' ============================================================================
package "Permission Ladder Mapping" as LadderMap {
    
    rectangle "Level 1: Situational" as L1 #90EE90
    note right of L1
        Auto-Grant: YES
        Scopes: chat.respond, catalog.browse, product.search
        Example: Chat interface, browse products
    end note
    
    rectangle "Level 2: Brand Trust" as L2 #FFFFE0
    note right of L2
        Auto-Grant: NO
        Scopes: preferences.save
        Example: Remember preferences
    end note
    
    rectangle "Level 3: Personal Relationship" as L3 #FFD700
    note right of L3
        Auto-Grant: NO (requires auth)
        Scopes: history.read, profile.personalize
        Example: Show order history
    end note
    
    rectangle "Level 4: Points Permission" as L4 #FFA500
    note right of L4
        Auto-Grant: NO (requires loyalty)
        Scopes: loyalty.read, offers.personalized
        Example: VIP pricing
    end note
    
    rectangle "Level 5: Agentic" as L5 #FF6B6B
    note right of L5
        Auto-Grant: NO (requires delegation)
        Scopes: orders.auto_create, payment.authorize
        Example: Auto-reorder with guardrails
    end note
    
    L1 -down-> L2 : Earns trust via\nrelevant content
    L2 -down-> L3 : Earns trust via\npersonalization value
    L3 -down-> L4 : Earns trust via\nexclusive benefits
    L4 -down-> L5 : Earns trust via\nconsistent reliability
    
    note right of L5
      Critical: Level 5 Guardrails
      - Product/category restrictions
      - Price caps & budget limits
      - Frequency constraints
      - Require confirmation if conditions change
      - Transparent audit trail
      - One-command revocation
      
      Without guardrails, delegation becomes
      unwanted automation (back to interruption!)
    end note
}

@enduml

' ============================================================================
' PART 2: PERMISSION ESCALATION FLOW (Sequence Diagram)
' ============================================================================
@startuml Permission Escalation Flow
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE

title Permission Escalation Flow: Coffee Auto-Reorder Journey

participant User
participant Agent
participant MCP
participant PermDB
participant AuditLog

group Session 1: First Visit (Situational)
note over User, Agent
  Level 1 permissions (chat interface, catalog browsing)
  are auto-granted on first interaction - no opt-in required.
  This is "situational" permission: user initiated contact.
end note

User -> Agent : [Opens chat interface]
Agent -> MCP : check_permission("chat.respond")
MCP -> PermDB : query scope="chat.respond"
PermDB --> MCP : GRANTED (Level 1, auto-granted)
MCP --> Agent : true
Agent -> User : "Hi! I can help you find products.\nWhat are you looking for today?"

User -> Agent : "Show me coffee beans"
Agent -> MCP : check_permission("catalog.browse")
MCP -> PermDB : query scope="catalog.browse"
PermDB --> MCP : GRANTED (Level 1, auto-granted)
MCP --> Agent : true
Agent -> User : [Displays catalog: Ethiopian, Colombian, Kenyan...]
User -> Agent : "I like this Ethiopian Yirgacheffe blend"
end

group Session 1: Permission Request (Brand Trust)
Agent -> MCP : check_permission("preferences.save")
MCP -> PermDB : query scope="preferences.save"
PermDB --> MCP : NOT GRANTED (Level 2, requires opt-in)
MCP --> Agent : false

Agent -> MCP : explain_permission("preferences.save")
MCP --> Agent : "Save user preferences to provide personalized recommendations"

Agent -> User : "May I remember your coffee preferences\nfor next time? I'll suggest similar options."
User -> Agent : "Sure"

Agent -> MCP : request_permission(\n  scope="preferences.save",\n  reason="personalized recommendations",\n  duration="1 year",\n  constraints=None\n)
MCP -> PermDB : INSERT permission_grant
MCP -> AuditLog : LOG(action=GRANT, level=2, source=verbal_consent)
PermDB --> MCP : permission_id="P123"
MCP --> Agent : PermissionGrant(id=P123, granted=true)

Agent -> Agent : Save preference: coffee_type="Ethiopian_Yirgacheffe"
Agent -> User : "Got it! I'll remember you love Ethiopian blends."
end

group Session 2: Two Weeks Later (Personal Relationship)
User -> Agent : "Hi, what did I order before?"

Agent -> MCP : check_permission("history.read")
MCP -> PermDB : query scope="history.read"
PermDB --> MCP : NOT GRANTED (Level 3, requires auth)
MCP --> Agent : false

Agent -> User : "Welcome back! To show your past orders,\nI need access to your purchase history."
User -> Agent : "Makes sense, go ahead"

Agent -> MCP : request_permission(\n  scope="history.read",\n  reason="display order history",\n  duration="permanent"\n)
MCP -> PermDB : INSERT permission_grant (requires auth confirmation)
MCP -> AuditLog : LOG(action=GRANT, level=3)
MCP --> Agent : PermissionGrant(id=P456, granted=true)

Agent -> Agent : Fetch order history
Agent -> User : "You've ordered Ethiopian Yirgacheffe 5 times\nin the last 3 months (Jan 15, Jan 28, Feb 5...)"
end

group Session 3: After 5 Purchases (Agentic Delegation)
Agent -> Agent : Detect pattern: monthly purchase of same product

Agent -> User : "I notice you buy Ethiopian Yirgacheffe\nevery month. Want me to automatically\nreorder when you're running low?"
User -> Agent : "How would that work exactly?"

Agent -> MCP : explain_permission("orders.auto_create")
MCP --> Agent : [Returns detailed explanation:\n- Monitors usage patterns\n- Orders 2 weeks before depletion\n- Respects price caps\n- Sends notification before ordering\n- Cancel anytime with one command]

Agent -> User : "I'll monitor your usage and order 2 weeks\nbefore you run out. Max $50/order, and I'll\nnotify you before each purchase.\nYou can cancel anytime by saying 'stop auto-ordering'."
User -> Agent : "Okay, but only that specific coffee,\nnot other products"

Agent -> MCP : request_permission(\n  scope="orders.auto_create",\n  reason="autonomous replenishment",\n  duration="until_revoked",\n  constraints={\n    "product_id": "ETH-YRG-001",\n    "max_price_usd": 50,\n    "frequency_limit": "monthly",\n    "require_notification": true\n  }\n)
MCP -> MCP : Validate constraints are present (REQUIRED for Level 5)
MCP -> PermDB : INSERT permission_grant with guardrails
MCP -> AuditLog : LOG(action=GRANT, level=5, constraints=explicit)
PermDB --> MCP : permission_id="P789"
MCP --> Agent : PermissionGrant(id=P789, granted=true)

Agent -> User : "Perfect! I'll take care of your coffee supply.\nYou can check or cancel anytime."
end

group Future: Autonomous Action (Agent Acts on Behalf)
Agent -> Agent : [2 weeks later] Detect: estimated 3 days of coffee left

Agent -> MCP : check_permission("orders.auto_create")
MCP -> PermDB : query permission P789, validate constraints
PermDB --> MCP : GRANTED with constraints
MCP --> Agent : true + constraints object

Agent -> Agent : Check current price: $42 (within $50 cap OK)
Agent -> Agent : Check last order: 28 days ago (monthly limit OK)
Agent -> Agent : Create order: Ethiopian Yirgacheffe 12oz
Agent -> User : [NOTIFICATION] "I've ordered your Ethiopian\nYirgacheffe ($42). Arrives Thursday."

Agent -> MCP : (Internal) log_permission_usage(P789)
MCP -> AuditLog : LOG(action=USE, permission=P789, outcome=order_created)
end

group Revocation Flow (User Withdraws Permission)
User -> Agent : "Stop auto-ordering coffee"

Agent -> MCP : revoke_permission("P789")
MCP -> PermDB : UPDATE permission_grant SET revoked_at=NOW()
MCP -> AuditLog : LOG(action=REVOKE, permission=P789, source=user_initiated)
PermDB --> MCP : success=true
MCP --> Agent : true

Agent -> Agent : Disable auto-order logic
Agent -> User : "Done! I've stopped auto-ordering.\nYou can always browse and order manually,\nand I'll still remember your preferences."

note over Agent
  Key Principle: Graceful Degradation
  Agent drops from Level 5 to Level 3
  (retains preferences & history,
  loses autonomous ordering).
  
  No relationship rupture - just
  adjusted permission scope.
end note
end

@enduml

' ============================================================================
' PART 3: PERMISSION RESOURCE SCHEMA (Data Model)
' ============================================================================
@startuml Permission Resource Schema
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE

title Permission Resource Schema

map "resource://permissions/user_789/current" as CurrentState {
    user_id => user_789
    agent_id => shopping_assistant
}

map "Permission Grant P123" as P123 #FFFFE0 {
    permission_id => P123
    level => 2 (Brand Trust)
    scope => preferences.save
}

map "Permission Grant P456" as P456 #FFD700 {
    permission_id => P456
    level => 3 (Personal)
    scope => history.read
}

map "Permission Grant P789" as P789 #FF6B6B {
    permission_id => P789
    level => 5 (Agentic)
    scope => orders.auto_create
}

map "Constraints for P789" as C789 {
    product_id => ETH-YRG-001
    max_price_usd => 50
    frequency_limit => monthly
}

map "Guardrails for P789" as G789 {
    require_confirmation => price changes
    auto_revoke => inactivity
    notify_on => order_created
}

CurrentState *-down- P123
CurrentState *-down- P456
CurrentState *-down- P789
P789 *-right- C789
P789 *-right- G789

rectangle "resource://permissions/user_789/ladder" as Ladder #LightBlue
note right of Ladder
  Shows available escalation paths
end note

map "Available Escalation Level 4" as L4Avail #FFA500 {
    level => 4 (Points)
    scope => loyalty.read
    value => VIP discounts
}

Ladder -down- L4Avail

rectangle "resource://permissions/user_789/audit" as Audit #LightGray
note right of Audit
  Permission history & compliance trail
end note

map "Audit Event 1" as E1 {
    event_id => E001
    action => GRANT
    permission_id => P123
}

map "Audit Event 5" as E5 {
    event_id => E005
    action => GRANT
    permission_id => P789
    level => 5
}

map "Audit Event 12" as E12 {
    event_id => E012
    action => USE
    permission_id => P789
}

Audit -down- E1
Audit -down- E5
Audit -down- E12

note bottom of Audit
  GDPR Article 30 Compliance
  Audit log serves as record of processing activities:
  - Legal basis for each permission (consent, contract, etc.)
  - Purpose limitation enforcement
  - Data subject rights (access, deletion, portability)
  - Automated decision-making transparency
  
  Retention: 7 years post-revocation
end note

@enduml

' ============================================================================
' PART 4: KEY INSIGHTS & INTEGRATION
' ============================================================================
@startuml Key Insights
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE

title Key Insights: From Interruption to Delegation

frame "Traditional Interruption Marketing" as OldWay #FFE4E1 {
    [Spray & Pray Email Blasts]
    [Cold Calls During Dinner]
    [Intrusive Pop-ups]
    [Auto-Play Video Ads]
    
    note bottom
      - Assumes right to interrupt
      - One-size-fits-all messaging
      - No earned relationship
      - High cost, low ROI
      - Breeds resentment
    end note
}

frame "Permission Marketing (Pre-AI)" as MidWay #FFFFE0 {
    [Opt-in Email Lists]
    [Marketing Automation\nJourneys]
    [Loyalty Programs]
    [Inbound Content]
    
    note bottom
      - Requests permission first
      - Segmented, personalized
      - Curriculum-based nurture
      - Customer controls relationship
      - Higher engagement
    end note
}

frame "Agentic AI + Permission Marketing" as NewWay #90EE90 {
    [Conversational Context\nNegotiation]
    [Dynamic Permission\nEscalation]
    [Autonomous Action\nwithin Guardrails]
    [Value-First Delegation]
    
    note bottom
      New Capabilities:
      - Real-time permission requests
      - Adaptive curriculum conversations
      - Transparent constraint setting
      - One-command revocation
      - Proactive value demonstration
      
      Result: Customer delegates
      decisions while maintaining control
    end note
}

OldWay -right-> MidWay : Shift 1\nFrom assumed\nto earned
MidWay -right-> NewWay : Shift 2\nFrom messages\nto actions

note as N1
  The Permission Marketing MCP enables:
  
  1. Multi-level consent modeling
     Map Seth Godin's ladder to technical scopes
  
  2. Conversational permission negotiation
     AI explains value, user grants/denies naturally
  
  3. Constraint-based delegation
     Level 5 requires explicit guardrails
  
  4. Graceful degradation on revocation
     Agent respects withdrawal without relationship rupture
  
  5. Audit trail for compliance & trust
     GDPR, transparency, accountability
  
  Core Principle:
  Permission is not a checkbox - it's an ongoing,
  renewable relationship asset that must be
  earned and can be lost.
end note

@enduml
